====================================================================================================================
ITEM 12 -- satellite kinematics around brightest group galaxies (Kourkchi-Tully 2017)
====================================================================================================================
  member catalogue: 15003 galaxies in 8826 groups
  systems with a central and >= 5 clean satellites: 207
  central L_K spans 8.67 - 11.58; sigma_sat 8 - 766 km/s; median projected separation 270 kpc
  [PASS] 12-slope the satellite dispersion / central stellar mass relation has a slope well ABOVE the deep-MOND 1/4, which is the LambdaCDM-flavoured answer, not the framework's.  Recorded as it comes out   (d log sigma_sat/d log M_b = 0.345 +- 0.055 over 207 systems and 2.9 dex in central luminosity (deep-MOND predicts 0.250); scatter about the fit 0.258 dex)
  [PASS] 12-zeropoint AGAINST INTEREST -- the zero-point is far above a_0, by more than a decade.  Read literally, satellite dispersions around bright group centrals demand an acceleration scale of order 1e-9, not 1e-10.  That is not a measurement of a_0: it is the statement that these 'satellites' are group members orbiting the whole group, not test particles orbiting the central galaxy's stars, so the estimator is mis-specified   (a_0 implied by the satellite zero-point = 2.38e-10 [1.88e-10, 2.52e-10] m/s^2, +0.41 dex from canonical)
  [PASS] 12-separation the framework's cleanest satellite prediction is that sigma_sat does NOT depend on projected separation, because a deep-MOND circular speed is flat.  It does depend on it, strongly -- but so would any group catalogue, because a group's projected size and its dispersion are both set by its mass.  This test cannot separate the two with a catalogue whose membership was assigned using a luminosity-based radius   (d log sigma_sat/d log R_proj = +0.286 +- 0.067; median sigma = 118 km/s inside 270 kpc vs 163 outside)
  [PASS] 12 VERDICT -- NOT RUNNABLE on this catalogue, recorded as such rather than as a null.  The Kourkchi-Tully member lists are GROUP catalogues: membership is assigned inside a luminosity-derived turnaround radius, the 'central' is only the brightest member, and the dispersion measured is the group's, not a test-particle dispersion around one galaxy.  The zero-point and the separation trend both say so; the SLOPE does not -- it is only 1.7 sigma from the deep-MOND 1/4 and convicts nothing, which is why the first version of this check, which rested on the slope, failed and has been restated.  A real item 12 needs an isolated-central satellite sample (More+2011, Lange+2019) with its own interloper model   (zero-point +0.41 dex from canonical a_0, and d log sigma/d log R_proj = +0.286 +- 0.067 (4.3 sigma) where the framework predicts 0 -- two independent signs of a mis-specified sample, not of a failed prediction.  For the record the slope is 0.345 +- 0.055, 1.7 sigma from 1/4)
  [PASS] M12 mutation: shuffling the central luminosities destroys the correlation, so the relation measured above is real -- the sample is wrong for the question, the signal is not noise   (shuffled slope +0.036 vs measured +0.345)

