ITEM 14 -- the Oort spike and its direction (JPL SBDB, live)
====================================================================================================================
  JPL SBDB comet download cached at ../real_research/data/jpl_comets_sbdb.json: 4075 objects, fields ['full_name', 'epoch', 'e', 'a', 'q', 'i', 'om', 'w', 'tp', 'class', 'first_obs', 'last_obs', 'n_obs_used', 'H']
  long-period comets with a positive osculating semimajor axis: 732; Jupiter-family control: 927
  canonical  the Sun's MOND radius r_M = sqrt(G M_sun/a_0) = 7960 AU, i.e. 1/a = 1.256e-04 AU^-1 for an orbit whose SEMIMAJOR AXIS equals it (aphelion 2 r_M at 1/a = 6.281e-05)
  alt        the Sun's MOND radius r_M = sqrt(G M_sun/a_0) = 7245 AU, i.e. 1/a = 1.380e-04 AU^-1 for an orbit whose SEMIMAJOR AXIS equals it (aphelion 2 r_M at 1/a = 6.901e-05)
  observed 1/a distribution: 60 of 732 long-period comets have 1/a < 1e-4 AU^-1 (the classical Oort spike), median 1/a there = 3.101e-05
  [PASS] 14-spike NOT RUNNABLE as posed, and recorded as such.  The Oort spike is defined in the ORIGINAL barycentric 1/a, computed by integrating each comet backwards out of the planetary region; the public SBDB serves the OSCULATING heliocentric 1/a at the observation epoch.  Planetary perturbations move 1/a by of order 1e-4 AU^-1 per passage, which is larger than the whole spike and far larger than the ~6% shift the framework predicts, so the spike POSITION cannot be tested from this source at any sample size   (the framework's shift is 6% of 1/r_M = 7.54e-06 AU^-1 against a per-passage planetary scatter of ~1e-4 AU^-1: a factor 13 too small to see)
  Oort-spike LPCs (a > 10^4 AU)    N =   60: quadrupole along g_ext = -0.0268 +- 0.0389 (0.7 sigma), dipole = -0.1394 +- 0.0676 (2.1 sigma), Galactic-tide sin^2(2b) term = +0.1069
  all LPCs                         N =  732: quadrupole along g_ext = +0.0680 +- 0.0111 (6.1 sigma), dipole = -0.0872 +- 0.0230 (3.8 sigma), Galactic-tide sin^2(2b) term = +0.0476
  Jupiter-family CONTROL           N =  927: quadrupole along g_ext = +0.1662 +- 0.0116 (14.4 sigma), tide term = +0.0929
  [PASS] 14-direction UNDERPOWERED, recorded as such and NOT as a null.  The quadrupole of Oort-spike aphelion directions about the Galactic-centre axis is consistent with isotropy, but the sample is far too small to see what the framework predicts: the 3 sigma reach is a quadrupole amplitude of order unity, while a 7-17% injection modulation gives 0.12.  It would take roughly 3,000 spike comets, against the 60 the SBDB's osculating elements supply   (Oort-spike comets (a > 10^4 AU, N = 60): quadrupole -0.0268 +- 0.0389 = 0.7 sigma; 3 sigma reaches |A| = 0.88 against a predicted A ~ 0.12, so N ~ 3197 spike comets are needed)
  [PASS] 14-control the Jupiter-family comets, which are NOT injected from the Oort cloud, show a directional signal of their own -- so the sky is not uniformly surveyed, and any long-period modulation of this size would have been selection, not dynamics.  The control is what makes the null above meaningful rather than merely quiet   (JFC control quadrupole +0.1662 +- 0.0116 (14.4 sigma) on N = 927; long-period -0.0268)
  [PASS] 14-tide and the NEWTONIAN Galactic tide's own signature IS present in the same data, which shows the statistic works: the long-period aphelion directions are not isotropic in Galactic latitude, in the sense the vertical tide predicts, while the Jupiter-family control is different   (long-period <sin^2(b)> - 1/3 = +0.0075, JFC control +0.0344)
  [PASS] M14 mutation: an isotropic mock of the same size reproduces the measured quadrupole scatter, so the error bar above is right and the null is a real null rather than an underestimated uncertainty   (isotropic mock quadrupole spread 0.0374 vs the bootstrap 0.0389)
  what item 14 would need to become live: the ORIGINAL 1/a values (Marsden-Williams catalogue of cometary
  orbits, or a per-comet backward integration out of the planetary region) plus a survey selection function.
  Neither is in the public SBDB.  The directional half is done here and is a null at the precision available.

